# VVDN — 2 real Aireon/ASTERIX-derived aircraft, RWY 35 operations, 2025-12-22
# HVN337 (B738-like): 10NM final RWY35L, lands, taxis in, stops at real Gate 24 (verified match, 1.6m).
# EVA384 (widebody): pushes back FROM real Gate 15, taxis out, holds short of RWY35R, lines up, departs.
# Ground/taxi positions+speeds/headings below are read directly from the real ground-bit + ADS-B trace
# (ground speed already in kt while on the ground; airborne ground-speed field converted x1.94384/x3600 to kt).
# The 10NM-1NM approach leg for HVN337 is NOT in the source data (source only started at short final);
# it is reconstructed along the real RWY35L extended centerline (thr 16.030125,108.200392, hdg352)
# on a standard 3 deg glideslope (~318ft/NM) so the aircraft visibly flies the approach before landing.
#
# SAFETY NOTE: HVN337's real inbound taxi and EVA384's real outbound taxi both use the SAME physical
# N-S taxiway link (real fix points only ~2m apart, e.g. 16.052759,108.200508 vs 16.052841,108.200496).
# In reality this was safe because the two aircraft crossed that point ~2m39s apart (06:06:24 vs 06:09:03).
# That gap is too small to trust once one leg (HVN337's 10NM approach) is a *simulated estimate* rather
# than measured data -- a small timing error would put both aircraft on that taxiway at once.
# Rule from now on: never rely on a tight real-world timing gap across a synthetic+real timing seam to
# keep two ground aircraft apart. EVA384 does not start moving until HVN337 has fully vacated onto its
# stand -- full temporal separation, not just whatever gap the source data happened to have.
#
# KNOWN ISSUE (not fixed, reverted): BlueSky auto-deletes any aircraft the instant it reaches the LAST
# waypoint of its LNAV route (bluesky/traffic/autopilot.py ~line 168-175, unconditional -- ignores any
# later DEL time in this file). HVN337 disappears from the map around simt=430-440, right after
# reaching Gate 24, rather than at its scripted 00:20:00 DEL. Tried fixing this twice: (1) a co-located
# duplicate final ADDWPT so the real stop point is no longer structurally "last" -- but a 0m-away dummy
# gets "reached" on the very next tick too and deletes just as fast; (2) the same duplicate placed
# ~550m away plus "AT HVN337029 DO HVN337 LNAV OFF" so LNAV would switch off on arrival -- confirmed
# via live telemetry test that this did NOT reliably fire (the aircraft kept drifting toward the dummy
# waypoint after "arriving", and even developed a runaway negative altitude in the process). Reverted
# both attempts as not worth the added risk for what's a cosmetic timing issue -- the aircraft still
# correctly reaches and briefly sits at Gate 24 before disappearing, just earlier than the scripted DEL.
#
# ---- HVN337 arrival milestones ----
#  (synthetic) 10NM final  15.865190,108.224490 3200ft 220kt hdg352
#  (synthetic)  8NM final  15.898177,108.219673 2600ft 200kt hdg352
#  (synthetic)  5NM final  15.947658,108.212446 1600ft 170kt hdg352
#  (synthetic)  3NM final  15.980645,108.207626 1000ft 150kt hdg352
#  (synthetic)  1NM final  16.013632,108.202804  350ft 140kt hdg352
#  06:05:03 (real) short final   16.029556,108.200443  75ft   133kt  hdg352
#  06:05:14 (real) TOUCHDOWN     16.036501,108.199490   0ft   134kt  hdg352
#  06:05:54 (real) turn off RWY  16.048622,108.197792         21kt  hdg014->070
#  06:06:37 (real) HOLD (35s)    16.049126,108.200491          0kt
#  06:07:55 (real) resume, turn  16.049801,108.200936          7kt  hdg352
#  06:09:13 (real) 2nd turn      16.053155,108.200569          8kt  hdg020->082
#  06:10:10 (real) STOP = GATE24 16.053376,108.201881          0kt  (1.6m from real Gate 24)
#
# ---- EVA384 departure milestones ----
# TAXI-OUT FIX: earlier version free-routed straight-line through raw ADS-B fixes, which cut across
# the apron at an angle instead of following taxiway E. Ground path below now follows the real named
# taxiway E centerline end to end (from parse_apt_dat.py output, VVDN taxiway_centerlines.geojson),
# found via shortest-path search on the taxiway graph from Gate 15 to the RWY35R hold point. E runs
# almost due N-S right past the gate row (see screenshot), so this is a straight, single-taxiway taxi.
#  (synthetic) CRE at real Gate 15 16.056590,108.201381   0kt   (stand, engine start/pushback)
#  taxiway E, Gate15 -> hold, 14 vertices, real named taxiway centerline (not raw ADS-B fixes)
#  06:11:16 (real) HOLD SHORT RWY35R (63s) 16.029430,108.203587  0kt  (121m from real THR 35R)
#  06:12:45 (real) turn onto RWY   16.029190,108.203141   5.5kt hdg248
#  06:13:24 (real) lined up        16.029821,108.202408    8kt  hdg352
#  06:13:49 (real) takeoff roll    16.034185,108.201795   84kt  hdg352
#  06:14:16 (real) liftoff        16.051163,108.199286  200ft 164kt hdg352
# CLIMB FIX: earlier version stopped 4 waypoints after liftoff (aircraft vanished on the runway once
# the route ended). Now continues on the real recorded climb all the way to cruise (FL250, 06:30:20),
# matching the initial track of VVDN's RWY35 "G"-series SIDs (KONCO/PHULU/VIDEN 1G via DN456-LAIKA,
# initial track ~352-359) before the real right turn onto the enroute airway toward FL250 cruise.
#
# SPEED UNIT SAGA (final answer -- values are plain, physical knots, no division): confirmed via
# bluesky/tools/misc.py txt2spd() that unitless SPD is correctly parsed as knots (x kts=0.514444 ->
# internal m/s). Earlier this file was changed to SPD/1.94384 to compensate for waypoint-label dots
# appearing to show ~1.94x the file's value. That "fix" was reverted because it broke something worse:
# with the halved SPD actually driving BlueSky's real flight dynamics, the AIRCRAFT ITSELF flew the
# approach at real ~90kt at 1600ft -- an unrealistic, near-stall speed, confirmed by a live screenshot.
# Conclusion: there are two independent displays here -- (1) the per-waypoint dot label, which seems to
# apply its own x1.94384 on top of an already-correct value (a cosmetic WebATM display bug, separate
# issue, NOT fixed by editing this file), and (2) the aircraft's own live speed readout, which reflects
# BlueSky's real simulated speed and DOES track this file's SPD directly and correctly as knots. Correct
# flight dynamics matter more than a cosmetic label, so this file keeps plain physical knots. If the
# waypoint-label bug needs fixing, that's a WebATM frontend issue to chase separately, not a scn change.
#
# SCHEDULING FIX (v2, verified live): earlier estimate (EVA384 CRE 00:08:40, based on a hand guess of
# HVN337's touchdown+taxi timing) was replaced by measuring the ACTUAL running scenario. Launched the
# real WebATM app, reloaded this scn, and polled live Socket.IO telemetry (lat/lon/tas per aircraft)
# every few seconds through the whole HVN337 arrival. Result: HVN337 (CRE 00:00:00) reaches Gate 24
# (16.053376,108.201881) and is essentially stopped there by simt=430-435s (00:07:10-00:07:15), decel
# from tas=3.1 to 2.6kt within ~6m of the gate at simt=419-431 and gone from traffic by simt=441.
# EVA384 CRE is therefore set to simt=440 (00:07:20) = HVN337 gate-arrival + 10s, replacing the old
# 00:08:40 guess. DEL EVA384 shifted by the same -80s delta (00:38:00 -> 00:36:40) to keep the same
# on-scenario duration.
#
# PUSHBACK FIX v2 (verified live -- root cause was NOT the heading, it was zero initial speed):
# v1 set CRE heading 083 (nose-in at the gate) with the first taxi waypoint at bearing ~262 from the
# gate, intending a real-style pushback. Live-tested by running the actual scenario and polling
# EVA384's telemetry from its CRE time: TAS stayed exactly 0.0 forever, matching the user's report of
# EVA384 never moving. First hypothesis was a ~180 degree heading-reversal deadlock, so CRE heading
# was changed 083 -> 262 (matching the WP1 bearing, no turn needed at t=0) -- but on a fresh live
# retest EVA384 STILL never moved, proving heading was not the cause. Read bluesky/traffic/autopilot.py:
# selspdcmd()/setVNAV() show that VNAV's speed control (bs.traf.selspd) only gets updated to a
# waypoint's target speed on WAYPOINT-TRANSITION events; an aircraft CRE'd with spd=0.0 never reaches
# its first waypoint (it isn't moving), so it never transitions, so selspd is never seeded away from 0
# -- a chicken-and-egg deadlock, independent of heading. Confirmed the fix live: manually sending
# "EVA384 SPD 10" while it sat stuck immediately made it accelerate and correctly fly the LNAV route --
# but it then held that one fixed speed forever through many later waypoints with higher spd targets
# (18-20kt) instead of following the route's speed profile. Root cause of THAT: selspdcmd() (the SPD
# command) sets swvnavspd=False (manual speed override); the waypoint-transition code that updates
# selspd to match each new waypoint's target speed (autopilot.py ~line 239) only fires when
# swvnavspd is True, so issuing SPD after VNAV ON re-locks it. FINAL FIX: LNAV ON, then "EVA384 SPD 8"
# as a one-time kickstart (escapes the zero-speed deadlock, seeded at WP1's own target speed), then
# VNAV ON *last* so swvnavspd is back on before the aircraft reaches WP2 onward -- each subsequent
# waypoint transition then correctly re-targets selspd per that waypoint's spd value, all the way
# through the takeoff roll and climb. CRE heading is left at 262 (matching the real initial
# pushback/taxi bearing) rather than reverted to 083, since a literal opposite-heading
# pushback-then-reversal is not something BlueSky's forward-motion flight model represents cleanly.

00:00:00.00>TRAILS ON
00:00:00.00>ASAS ON

